<% set_meta_tags(
  title: "About",
  description: "WaterLevels.org maps public water gauges and turns streamflow, water level, and temperature readings into clear views for anyone who needs them."
) %>
<div class="app-shell">
  <%= render Navigation::BarComponent.new %>

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="glow" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="blob-a"></div>
      <div class="blob-b"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="inner">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> About WaterLevels.org</span>
      <h1>
        Making water data
        <span class="accent">accessible</span>
        to everyone
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        WaterLevels.org maps USGS monitoring locations plus selected Bureau of Reclamation, U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, National Weather Service (NWPS), and California Data Exchange Center gauges, and turns streamflow, water level, and temperature readings into clear, usable views for anyone who needs them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main id="main" class="page-main narrow">
    <div class="prose-page">
      <section aria-labelledby="mission-heading">
        <h2 id="mission-heading">Our mission</h2>
        <p>
          USGS collects invaluable hydrological data, but the raw feeds can be hard to browse. WaterLevels.org bridges that gap with an interactive map, station pages, and recent history drawn directly from official monitoring stations.
        </p>
        <p>
          Whether you are checking a local river, comparing nearby gauges, or exploring conditions across a state, the goal is the same: clear access to public water data with full attribution.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="data-heading">
        <h2 id="data-heading">Data sources</h2>
        <p>
          Nearly all stream values come from the USGS Water Data APIs. Selected Reclamation reservoirs (such as Lake Mead) use daily elevations from USBR RISE. Selected Corps projects (such as Raystown Lake) use pool elevations from the USACE CWMS Data API. Selected NWPS gauges without a USGS site id publish observed stage from the National Water Prediction Service. Selected California reservoirs (such as Lake Oroville) use daily elevations from CDEC. Provisional readings can change as agencies review observations. For the authoritative record at any station, use the official agency page linked from each gauge.
        </p>
        <p>
          <%= link_to "Read our full data disclosures", disclosures_path %> for what we show, how we cache updates, and important limitations.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>

  <%= render Footer::ThinComponent.new %>
</div>
